.TH UPSEM 2
.SH NAME
upsem, downsem, altsems, semstats, semsleep, semwakeup, semalt \- optimistic user level semaphores
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.PP
.B
void upsem(int *s);
.PP
.B
int downsem(int *s, int dontblock);
.PP
.B
int altsems(int *ss[], int n);
.PP
.B
void semstats(void)
.PP
.B extern int semtrytimes;
.br
.B extern int semdebug;
.PP
.B
int semsleep(int *s, int dontblock);
.PP
.B
void semwakeup(int *s);
.PP
.B
int semalt(int *ss[], int n);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IR Upsem ,
.IR downsem ,
and
.I altsems
provide an interface for optimistic semaphores that work without entering the
kernel when they can proceed, and call the kernel only when it is really necessary
(e.g., to block or to unblock another process).
They come from the NIX kernel.
.PP
A semaphore is an integer kept at memory, shared among synchronizing processes
(see
.B RFMEM
in
.IR fork (2)
and
.IR segattach (2)).
Initialization is done by the user program using either zero or a positive number. After that,
only these functions should be used to operate on it.
.PP
.I Downsem
tries to acquire one unit from the semaphore. If it can proceed, the call works without
calling the kernel. When it cannot proceed, the global
.I semtrytimes
(100)
controls for how long (how many times) the function will try to acquire without entering the
kernel, yielding the processor each time
.RI ( sleep (2)
with 0).
If this fails, the kernel is entered to block the process until it can be acquired.
If
.I dontblock
is set,
.I downsem
returns \-1 instead of blocking.
It returns 0 when it got the unit.
.PP
.I Upsem
releases one unit into the semaphore. If a process is waiting, it will take it and proceed.
Otherwise, the unit is kept in the semaphore for further
.I downsem
calls. The call does not enter the kernel unless a process must be awaken.
.PP
.I Altsems
tries to perform a
.I downsem
in any one of the semaphores pointed to by pointers in
.I ss
(there are
.I n
entries in that array, at most 16). If no semaphore can be acquired, and the operation has been
attempted for
.IR semtrytimes ,
the kernel is entered and the process blocks until it can proceed. Otherwise, the
operation is performed without calling the kernel.
It returns the index in
.I ss
of the semaphore acquired.
.PP
The semaphore's value is negative when there are processes waiting for it,
and zero or positive otherwise.
.PP
.I Semstats
prints several statistics for debugging, and may be useful to learn if the
processes using the semaphores (any semaphore) did enter the kernel or not and
how many times they did.
With
.I semdebug
set, the functions print what they do.
.PP
.IR Semsleep ,
.IR semwakeup ,
and
.I semalt
are the system calls behind them, for their use only.
.I Semsleep
waits for a unit after a decrement made the semaphore negative,
or, with
.IR dontblock ,
undoes the decrement and returns \-1;
.I semwakeup
gives the units an increment made to the processes waiting;
.I semalt
decrements one of the semaphores, waiting if none has a unit,
and returns its index.
.PP
The kernel knows a semaphore by its segment and address;
processes sharing one must have the segment at the same address.
The semaphores are independent of those of
.IR semacquire (2).
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/libc/9sys/upsem.c
and
.B /sys/src/9/port/syssem.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR fork (2),
.IR lock (2),
.IR rendezvous (2),
.IR segattach (2),
.IR semacquire (2),
.IR thread (2),
.IR tsend (2),
.IR tube (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A note interrupts a process waiting in the kernel:
.I downsem
and
.I altsems
then return \-1 and set
.IR errstr ;
the unit the process waited for is not taken.
A semaphore at an address that is not writable user memory
is an error, as for other system calls.
On 386 the system calls fail
.RB ( "no NIX semaphores" ).
